* Clojure

** Immutability
***_ Clojure uses an immutable data model;\nwe return new data structures with\nchanges applied instead of mutating data

*** assoc
****_ Associates a key with a value in an\nassociative structure (usually a map)\nand returns a new structure

*** conj
****_ Adds an element (conjoins) to a\ncollection and returns a new collection
*****_ Vectors: adds at the end
*****_ Lists: adds to front
*****_ Sets: adds if not already present
*****_ Maps: adds map entries

*** conj vs assoc on maps
****_ assoc sets a specific key —\nuse when you know the key
****_ conj adds map entries —\nuse when adding entries generically

***_ Clojure is dynamically typed but conj\non maps requires the value to be\na valid map entry

***_ Clojure does not fully copy collections\non conj/assoc — it uses persistent data\nstructures with structural sharing.\nMost of the old structure is reused,\nonly small parts are new, and updates\nare cheap. Old state still exists as a\nvalue; if no longer referenced it\nbecomes eligible for GC.

** Recursion
***_ The idea in Clojure is that loop state\nshould be explicit and values should\nremain immutable

***_ Plain recursion can blow the stack\nand cause a StackOverflowError

*** Tail Call Optimization
****_ Clojure does not automatically\nperform Tail Call Optimization

****_ A call is in tail position when it is\nthe final action of the function.\nBecause nothing happens after the\nrecursive call, the compiler can:\nreuse the current stack frame,\navoid stack growth, and run in\nconstant stack space.

**** recur
*****_ recur is a control structure that\njumps back to the nearest loop or\nfunction entry with new bindings;\nit cannot jump into another function

*****_ recur helps to prevent any\naccidental non-tail recursion

** Destructuring
***_ Deconstructing in Clojure is a binding mechaninsm that lets you pull vallues from a data structure like maps, vectors, etc. at the moment you bind a name, instead of manually callling get or nth. In practice, it is most often usedd in function parameter lists and let
***_ Deconstructing allows you to extract values from key value pairs by key from maps and bind them to local names
***_ :keys binds local names to the values of map keys with the same name
***_ :as keeps a binding to the entire original map.

** Higher Order Functions
***_ HOFs are functions that treat fuctions as data, this allows abstracting of patterns of computation
***_ Concept: Functions are values, so they can be passed to other functions like map

** Anonymous Functions (lambdas)
***_ An anonymous function can reference local bindings from the environment where it was created, these bindings are retained for execution

**Sequences
***_ A sequence in Clojure is an interface, not the data
***_ A sequence (seq) is a logical view over things you can iterate. This works for many things sucvh as lists, vectors, sets, maps; strings; lazy computations
*** How to get next element:
****_ seq returns a seq view, or nil if empty, used to test “is there anything to consume?”
****_ first returns first element of a seq (or nil)
****_ rest returns “everything after first”, returns an empty seq () when nothing remains (not nil)
****_ next like rest, but returns nil when nothing remains; often used when you want a nil termination condition

***_ Most sequences (map, filter, remove, etc.) are lazy, meaning they are computed on demand
*** ->> (thread last)
****_ ->> takes an expression and inserts it as the last argument of the next form, improves readability for sequence transformations because most sequence functions take the collection as the last argument.

